---
// /lifecycle — the chain of custody, long-form.
//
// The longest and most credible route, chosen deliberately: this page's job is
// not to convert quickly but to be the thing a sceptical reader finishes and
// believes. Every station names a real endpoint and a real actor, so the page
// can be checked rather than taken on trust.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import stations from "../data/stations.json";

const title = "The chain of custody — eight stations | Odal Node";
const description =
  "One record, eight stations: declare, evidence, check, seal, resolve, disclose, hand over, end. Each with the actor who touches it and the endpoint that serves them.";

const unmatched = stations.stations.filter((s) => s.unmatched);
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section eyebrow="Chain of custody" title="A passport is not a document. It is a record with a life." wide>
    <p>
      Most descriptions of a product passport stop at publication — import,
      validate, sign, publish. That is the first half. A product is repaired,
      resold, remanufactured and eventually recycled, and the record has to
      survive all of it while answering different people differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Eight stations. Each names the actor who touches the record there, and the
      endpoint that serves them.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="The spine" title="One record, eight stations." tone="muted" wide>
    <div class="not-prose space-y-5">
      {stations.stations.map((s) => (
        <article
          id={s.key}
          class={`card p-6 ${s.unmatched ? "card-emphasis" : ""}`}
        >
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-neutral-400">
              {String(s.n).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">{s.title}</h3>
            <span class="text-sm text-neutral-500">{s.who}</span>
            {s.unmatched && (
              <span class="rounded bg-primary-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold text-primary-800">
                No competitor equivalent
              </span>
            )}
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-base font-medium text-neutral-900">{s.lead}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">{s.body}</p>
          <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-neutral-500">{s.endpoint}</p>
          {s.link && (
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
              <a href={s.link} class="link-arrow text-primary-700">
                Operate this one yourself
              </a>
            </p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="The gap" title="Most tools stop at station five." wide>
    <p>
      Import, validate, sign, publish, resolve — that is a passport generator,
      and it is genuinely useful. It is also where the difficult half begins.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stations {unmatched.map((s) => s.n).join(", ")} are the ones that make a
      record survive a decade of ownership changes rather than describing a
      product on the day it was made:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {unmatched.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{s.title}</strong> — {s.lead}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      They are the reason this page is long. A capability that only shows up
      three years after purchase cannot be demonstrated in a screenshot, so the
      alternative is to describe it precisely enough to be checked.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Status" title="What is built, stated plainly." tone="tinted">
    <p>
      All eight stations exist in the software today. <strong>Nobody is running
      it in production</strong> — no customers, no pilots, no live deployments.
      The battery passport obligation begins on 18 February 2027, and we would
      rather be correct when it arrives than early and wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code is open:{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/odal-node/dpp-core">dpp-core</a> is Apache-2.0
      and can be read, audited or reused by anyone, including a competitor.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>
